notes.body:
This seems a lot simpler than you are making it to be. You can use this function instead:

```
static inline void
alloc_address_uint64(scope, address *addr, int addr_type, guint64 value)
{
	alloc_address_wmem(scope, addr, addr_type, sizeof(guint64), &value);
}
```

Then your `numeric_addr_to_str()` function just needs to print a guint64. Assume it receives numbers in host byte order and ignore endianness.

I'm not sure I understand the question. When you pass an unsigned number by value it works, whatever the width. This is OK:

```
    address *src = wmem_alloc0(pinfo->pool, sizeof(address));
    guint16 receiver = 0x3FF;
    alloc_address_uint64(pinfo->pool, src, AT_NUMERIC, receiver);
```

What's the motivation for requiring AT_NUMERIC addresses in little-endian?  It's not host byte order and not TCP/IP network byte order so I don't understand the rationale used here.

Is this code correct for big-endian machines? Seems like 0x3FF needs to be converted to little-endian first no?

```
    if (osinfo->msg_type == OPENSAFETY_SPDO_MESSAGE_TYPE)
        receiver = 0x3FF;
     alloc_address_wmem(pinfo->pool, dst, AT_NUMERIC, (int) sizeof(guint16), &receiver);
```

